From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive <dev@ceralive.tv>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 22:45:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 25/26] media: rockchip: rkvenc: keep the service alive until its last descriptor closes

MOTIVATION. A KASAN+lockdep Rock 5B+ reports 'BUG: KASAN:
slab-use-after-free in __mutex_lock' from
rkvenc_dev_release+0x100 the moment userspace closes a
descriptor it held across an mpp-srv unbind. The freed object
is srv itself: it is devm-allocated on the service's platform
device, so devres_release_all() frees it the moment
rkvenc_service_remove() returns, and release() then takes
mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock) on it.

remove() exists to prevent exactly that, and
RKVENC_QUIESCE_TIMEOUT_MS's own comment states teardown 'does
NOT proceed to release state those descriptors can still
reach'. Two gaps made that untrue. On a SERVICE-node unbind the
cores unbind FIRST -- __device_release_driver() runs
device_links_unbind_consumers() before device_remove() -- so
the first core's rkvenc_service_quiesce_for_core() owns the
LIVE -> QUIESCING transition, and the service's own
rkvenc_service_quiesce() gets RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER and
returns without waiting even once. That is the NORMAL path on a
service unbind, not an edge case. And when it did own the
transition it discarded RKVENC_DRAIN_TIMED_OUT, so 'quiesce
timed out ... refusing to release service state' printed and
the release went ahead regardless.

A third defect sits in the same drain.
rkvenc_service_sessions_gone() took session_lock, and every
caller is a wait_event*() condition -- which ___wait_event()
evaluates AFTER prepare_to_wait_event() has set the task state.
The board prints 'WARNING: kernel/sched/core.c:9091 at
__might_sleep' from rkvenc_service_drain+0x260. It had never
fired before because no run had reached the wait loop with a
session still open.

BEHAVIOUR. srv stops being devm-allocated and becomes reference
counted, anchored on a struct device it now EMBEDS and
publishes with cdev_device_add(); that device's release() frees
srv. The driver binding holds one reference and every open file
holds another, so teardown may RETURN with a descriptor open
without the object going anywhere. The kobject parenting
cdev_device_add() sets up is what covers __fput()'s cdev_put(),
which runs AFTER ->release() and which no explicit put of ours
could ever reach.

remove() then quiesces and WAITS -- unbounded and
interruptible. Unbounded because a bounded wait that expires is
a wait that then frees reachable state; the 10 s bound stays
where it belongs, on the TRANSIENT per-core drain, which has
somewhere to recover to. Interruptible because remove() runs in
the context of whoever wrote the sysfs unbind attribute, and an
uninterruptible wait parks that writer in D state where no
signal reaches it -- while the process tree that would close
the descriptor is typically the one blocked on that writer.
That is a real deadlock, not a slow unbind. NOT_OWNER stops
being a reason to skip anything.

Reference counting srv alone would only move the
use-after-free to the core: struct rkvenc_dev is devm-allocated
on the CORE's device, and a surviving session reaches it
through session->mpp and task->mpp --
rkvenc_free_task_callback() decrements mpp->task_count,
rkvenc_task_finalize() takes mpp->iommu_info's rwsem, and
rkvenc_task_timeout_work() calls disable_irq(mpp->irq) up to
two seconds after a task that was running when the core went
away. So a core's unwind SEVERS those pointers, at the one
point where its IRQ is already silent and the worker already
drained, and every consumer treats the NULL as nothing left to
release -- the same explicit, checkable fact 0020 made of
sub_devices[] and 0024 of iommu_info->domain. The DMA session
additionally takes a reference on the device it attached to,
because its dma-buf detach outlives that core.

NON-GOALS. Does not change the happy path: with no descriptor
open the wait is a predicate test that returns immediately, and
an idle unbind still completes in under a second. Does not
change the TRANSIENT per-core drain's bound or its LIVE/DEAD
outcome, which are 0020's. Does not touch task reference
counting, so 0021 and 0023 are unaffected -- no kref_get() is
added to hold a window open. Does NOT reclaim a task abandoned
on queue->pending_list by an abort: it keeps its allocator
reference and is never freed, so it is a leak on a teardown
path and NOT a use-after-free, and fixing it means changing the
reference model 0023 just stabilised. Does NOT fix the
'possible recursive locking' warning on &rg->rw_sem, which is a
separate tracked defect.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive fix. 'A devm-allocated service
torn down under open file descriptors' is claimed fixed by
0014's own status row, and that claim holds only while the
drain COMPLETES; the NOT_OWNER early return is 0020's as
written, and the mutex inside the wait condition is 0014's. The
ledger rows for both are corrected with this patch. No upstream
counterpart exists.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Before/after on ONE Rock 5B+, the same
KASAN+PROVE_LOCKING kernel and the same harness, with only the
module swapped. Before: tests/rkvenc-unbind.sh --states
timeout-negative FAILS with 'unbind COMPLETED with a file
descriptor still open', the KASAN slab-use-after-free above,
and the __might_sleep WARNING. After: that fixture PASSES, the
log shows 'teardown waiting for 1 open file descriptor(s)'
followed by 'teardown wait interrupted ... the service outlives
this unbind on its reference count', and the sanitizer sweep is
zero. All four states -- idle, held-open-fd, inflight,
timeout-negative -- pass, 20 unbind/rebind cycles complete, and
every encode returns the canonical 1,854,524 bytes. The sever
is covered separately, because the harness cannot reach it: its
FD holder never issues an ioctl, so its session never attaches
to a core. No test expectation changes. See
docs/UPSTREAM-STATUS.md and docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.1.7, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0025-rkvenc-service-node-teardown-lifetime.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_drv.c
@@ -524,6 +524,17 @@ static void rkvenc_core_unwind(struct rkvenc_dev *enc)
 		kthread_flush_worker(&queue->worker);
 	enc->cur_task = NULL;
 
+	/*
+	 * The IRQ is silent and the worker is drained, so nothing can still be
+	 * mid-dispatch on this core -- which makes this the first moment it is
+	 * safe to sever, and the last moment it is still useful. Everything
+	 * below frees state a session that outlived the drain can otherwise
+	 * reach through session->mpp, and the drain above is allowed to give up
+	 * (it is the bounded, transient flavour) so "the sessions are gone" is
+	 * not something this path may assume.
+	 */
+	rkvenc_service_detach_sessions(srv, enc);
+
 	/* 6. SRAM/IOMMU mapping owned by this core. */
 	if (test_and_clear_bit(RKVENC_STAGE_SRAM_MAPPED, &enc->probe_stages) &&
 	    enc->iommu_info && enc->iommu_info->domain) {
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_hw.h
@@ -461,12 +461,36 @@ enum rkvenc_service_state {
 };
 
 /* ---- MPP service ---- */
+/*
+ * LIFETIME. srv is NOT devm-allocated, and that is the whole point: devres
+ * frees a devm allocation the instant remove() returns, while an open file
+ * descriptor keeps reaching srv long after that -- rkvenc_dev_release() takes
+ * srv->session_lock, and __fput() then calls cdev_put() on the cdev EMBEDDED
+ * below. Neither is optional and neither can be made to happen earlier.
+ *
+ * So the object is reference counted, and child_dev -- the /dev/mpp_service
+ * device, now embedded rather than allocated by device_create() -- is the
+ * counter. Its ->release() frees srv. Two independent references keep it alive:
+ *
+ *   1. the DRIVER BINDING, taken by device_initialize() in the probe and
+ *      dropped by put_device() at the end of rkvenc_service_remove();
+ *   2. one per OPEN FILE, taken in rkvenc_dev_open() and dropped last in
+ *      rkvenc_dev_release().
+ *
+ * (2) is deliberately belt-and-braces: cdev_device_add() sets the cdev
+ * kobject's PARENT to &child_dev.kobj and cdev_add() takes a reference on it,
+ * so the reference chrdev_open() holds on the cdev already pins srv all the way
+ * through __fput()'s cdev_put() -- which is the one place an explicit put of
+ * ours could never cover, because it runs AFTER ->release() returns. That chain
+ * is the load-bearing one; (2) makes the session -> srv edge an explicit fact
+ * rather than an inference about char-device internals.
+ */
 struct rkvenc_service {
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct cdev mpp_cdev;
 	dev_t dev_id;
 	struct class *cls;
-	struct device *child_dev;
+	struct device child_dev;
 
 	u32 taskqueue_cnt;
 	struct rkvenc_taskqueue *task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_BUTT];
@@ -490,6 +514,8 @@ struct rkvenc_service {
 bool rkvenc_service_is_live(struct rkvenc_service *srv);
 void rkvenc_service_quiesce(struct rkvenc_service *srv);
 void rkvenc_service_quiesce_for_core(struct rkvenc_service *srv);
+void rkvenc_service_detach_sessions(struct rkvenc_service *srv,
+				    struct rkvenc_dev *enc);
 
 /* ---- MPP task ---- */
 struct rkvenc_mpp_task {
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_iommu.c
@@ -281,6 +281,8 @@ int rkvenc_dma_session_destroy(struct rkvenc_dma_session *dma)
 		kref_put(&buffer->ref, rkvenc_dma_release_buffer);
 	mutex_unlock(&dma->list_mutex);
 
+	/* Paired with the get in rkvenc_dma_session_create(). */
+	put_device(dma->dev);
 	kfree(dma);
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -312,7 +314,13 @@ rkvenc_dma_session_create(struct device *dev, u32 max_buffers)
 		INIT_LIST_HEAD(&buffer->link);
 		list_add_tail(&buffer->link, &dma->unused_list);
 	}
-	dma->dev = dev;
+	/*
+	 * A session outlives the core's unbind, and every dma-buf attachment it
+	 * still has to detach names this device. Holding it explicitly is what
+	 * makes "the core went away, the session did not" a safe state rather
+	 * than a bet on the platform device happening to stick around.
+	 */
+	dma->dev = get_device(dev);
 
 	return dma;
 }
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
@@ -1434,9 +1434,12 @@ static int rkvenc_dev_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
 	}
 	list_add_tail(&session->service_link, &srv->session_list);
 	session->index = atomic_inc_return(&srv->sub_devices[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]->session_index);
-	srv->session_count++;
+	WRITE_ONCE(srv->session_count, srv->session_count + 1);
 	mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
 
+	/* Paired with the put at the very end of rkvenc_dev_release(). */
+	get_device(&srv->child_dev);
+
 	filp->private_data = session;
 
 	return nonseekable_open(inode, filp);
@@ -1468,7 +1471,7 @@ static int rkvenc_dev_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
 		mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
 		list_del_init(&session->service_link);
 		if (srv->session_count)
-			srv->session_count--;
+			WRITE_ONCE(srv->session_count, srv->session_count - 1);
 		mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
 	}
 
@@ -1517,6 +1520,16 @@ static int rkvenc_dev_release(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
 		if (!srv->session_count)
 			wake_up_all(&srv->quiesce_wait);
 		mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
+
+		/*
+		 * LAST, and the ordering is the fix: everything above still
+		 * reaches srv, and on the final close of a service that has
+		 * already been unbound this is the reference that was keeping
+		 * it allocated. __fput()'s cdev_put() runs after this returns
+		 * and reaches srv too, which is why the cdev's own reference on
+		 * &srv->child_dev -- not this put -- is what makes that safe.
+		 */
+		put_device(&srv->child_dev);
 	}
 
 	return 0;
@@ -1538,15 +1551,31 @@ const struct file_operations rkvenc_fops = {
  */
 #define RKVENC_QUIESCE_TIMEOUT_MS 10000
 
+/*
+ * Deliberately LOCKLESS. Every caller is a wait_event*() condition, and
+ * ___wait_event() evaluates its condition AFTER prepare_to_wait_event() has
+ * already set the task state -- so taking a mutex here is a blocking operation
+ * with !TASK_RUNNING, which CONFIG_DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP (on in the edge-test
+ * kernel) reports and which is a genuine mistake, not just a noisy one. A
+ * single u32 read needs no lock anyway: the writers update it under
+ * session_lock and then wake, and wait_event()'s own barriers pair with that.
+ */
 static bool rkvenc_service_sessions_gone(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
 {
-	bool gone;
+	return READ_ONCE(srv->session_count) == 0;
+}
 
-	mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
-	gone = (srv->session_count == 0);
-	mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
+/*
+ * The counter behind struct rkvenc_service's lifetime. Reached only once the
+ * driver binding and every open file descriptor have let go, in either order.
+ */
+static void rkvenc_service_dev_release(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct rkvenc_service *srv =
+		container_of(dev, struct rkvenc_service, child_dev);
 
-	return gone;
+	mutex_destroy(&srv->session_lock);
+	kfree(srv);
 }
 
 /* ---- Service probe/remove ---- */
@@ -1555,10 +1584,20 @@ int rkvenc_service_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	int ret;
 	struct rkvenc_service *srv;
 	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct task_struct *kworker;
+	struct class *cls;
+	dev_t dev_id;
 	u32 taskqueue_cnt = 0;
 	u32 resetgroup_cnt = 0;
 
-	srv = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*srv), GFP_KERNEL);
+	/*
+	 * kzalloc, NOT devm_kzalloc: see the lifetime note on struct
+	 * rkvenc_service. Everything below that IS devm-allocated -- the task
+	 * queue and the reset group -- is unreachable from a file descriptor
+	 * once rkvenc_service_remove() has returned, so it may keep dying with
+	 * the binding.
+	 */
+	srv = kzalloc(sizeof(*srv), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!srv)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
@@ -1581,8 +1620,10 @@ int rkvenc_service_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		struct rkvenc_taskqueue *queue;
 
 		queue = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*queue), GFP_KERNEL);
-		if (!queue)
-			return -ENOMEM;
+		if (!queue) {
+			ret = -ENOMEM;
+			goto err_free;
+		}
 
 		mutex_init(&queue->session_lock);
 		spin_lock_init(&queue->pending_lock);
@@ -1605,7 +1646,9 @@ int rkvenc_service_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 						  "rkvenc-worker");
 		if (IS_ERR(queue->kworker_task)) {
 			dev_err(dev, "failed to create kthread worker\n");
-			return PTR_ERR(queue->kworker_task);
+			ret = PTR_ERR(queue->kworker_task);
+			queue->kworker_task = NULL;
+			goto err_free;
 		}
 
 		srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC] = queue;
@@ -1616,8 +1659,10 @@ int rkvenc_service_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		struct rkvenc_reset_group *rg;
 
 		rg = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*rg), GFP_KERNEL);
-		if (!rg)
-			return -ENOMEM;
+		if (!rg) {
+			ret = -ENOMEM;
+			goto err_worker;
+		}
 
 		init_rwsem(&rg->rw_sem);
 		rg->rw_sem_on = true;
@@ -1628,31 +1673,45 @@ int rkvenc_service_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	ret = alloc_chrdev_region(&srv->dev_id, 0, 1, MPP_SERVICE_NAME);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(dev, "alloc_chrdev_region failed: %d\n", ret);
-		return ret;
-	}
-
-	cdev_init(&srv->mpp_cdev, &rkvenc_fops);
-	srv->mpp_cdev.owner = THIS_MODULE;
-
-	ret = cdev_add(&srv->mpp_cdev, srv->dev_id, 1);
-	if (ret) {
-		dev_err(dev, "cdev_add failed: %d\n", ret);
-		goto err_cdev;
+		goto err_worker;
 	}
 
 	srv->cls = class_create(MPP_CLASS_NAME);
 	if (IS_ERR(srv->cls)) {
 		ret = PTR_ERR(srv->cls);
+		srv->cls = NULL;
 		dev_err(dev, "class_create failed: %d\n", ret);
-		goto err_class;
+		goto err_chrdev;
 	}
 
-	srv->child_dev = device_create(srv->cls, dev, srv->dev_id,
-				       NULL, MPP_SERVICE_NAME);
-	if (IS_ERR(srv->child_dev)) {
-		ret = PTR_ERR(srv->child_dev);
-		dev_err(dev, "device_create failed: %d\n", ret);
-		goto err_device;
+	/*
+	 * device_create() would allocate a struct device whose release()
+	 * kfree()s only that device, which is no use as a lifetime anchor for
+	 * srv. Own it instead, and hand it to cdev_device_add() so the cdev's
+	 * kobject parents onto it -- that parenting is what keeps srv alive
+	 * across __fput()'s cdev_put().
+	 *
+	 * From device_initialize() onwards the ONLY legal way to free srv is
+	 * put_device(), so every error path below goes through it.
+	 */
+	device_initialize(&srv->child_dev);
+	srv->child_dev.class = srv->cls;
+	srv->child_dev.parent = dev;
+	srv->child_dev.devt = srv->dev_id;
+	srv->child_dev.release = rkvenc_service_dev_release;
+	ret = dev_set_name(&srv->child_dev, MPP_SERVICE_NAME);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dev, "dev_set_name failed: %d\n", ret);
+		goto err_put_device;
+	}
+
+	cdev_init(&srv->mpp_cdev, &rkvenc_fops);
+	srv->mpp_cdev.owner = THIS_MODULE;
+
+	ret = cdev_device_add(&srv->mpp_cdev, &srv->child_dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dev, "cdev_device_add failed: %d\n", ret);
+		goto err_put_device;
 	}
 
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, srv);
@@ -1660,12 +1719,23 @@ int rkvenc_service_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 
 	return 0;
 
-err_device:
-	class_destroy(srv->cls);
-err_class:
-	cdev_del(&srv->mpp_cdev);
-err_cdev:
+err_put_device:
+	cls = srv->cls;
+	dev_id = srv->dev_id;
+	kworker = srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]->kworker_task;
+	/* Frees srv through rkvenc_service_dev_release(). */
+	put_device(&srv->child_dev);
+	class_destroy(cls);
+	unregister_chrdev_region(dev_id, 1);
+	kthread_stop(kworker);
+	return ret;
+err_chrdev:
 	unregister_chrdev_region(srv->dev_id, 1);
+err_worker:
+	kthread_stop(srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]->kworker_task);
+err_free:
+	mutex_destroy(&srv->session_lock);
+	kfree(srv);
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -1686,6 +1756,34 @@ enum rkvenc_drain_result {
 	RKVENC_DRAIN_COMPLETE,		/* every open file reached release() */
 };
 
+/*
+ * Abort every in-flight submission and drain the worker. Idempotent and
+ * ownership-free on purpose: it is correct to run it again on a service
+ * somebody else has already started quiescing, and the permanent teardown path
+ * below depends on being able to do exactly that.
+ */
+static void rkvenc_service_abort_all(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
+{
+	struct rkvenc_session *session;
+
+	mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
+	list_for_each_entry(session, &srv->session_list, service_link)
+		rkvenc_session_abort(session);
+	mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
+
+	/*
+	 * Cancel and drain everything already queued or in flight BEFORE
+	 * waiting on userspace: a waiter cannot reach release() while its task
+	 * is still parked in the worker.
+	 */
+	if (srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]) {
+		struct rkvenc_taskqueue *queue = srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC];
+
+		if (queue->kworker_task)
+			kthread_flush_worker(&queue->worker);
+	}
+}
+
 /*
  * LIVE -> QUIESCING, then drain. Shared by both quiesce flavours.
  *
@@ -1701,29 +1799,15 @@ enum rkvenc_drain_result {
  */
 static enum rkvenc_drain_result rkvenc_service_drain(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
 {
-	struct rkvenc_session *session;
-
 	mutex_lock(&srv->session_lock);
 	if (srv->state != RKVENC_SRV_LIVE) {
 		mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
 		return RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER;
 	}
 	srv->state = RKVENC_SRV_QUIESCING;
-	list_for_each_entry(session, &srv->session_list, service_link)
-		rkvenc_session_abort(session);
 	mutex_unlock(&srv->session_lock);
 
-	/*
-	 * Cancel and drain everything already queued or in flight BEFORE
-	 * waiting on userspace: a waiter cannot reach release() while its task
-	 * is still parked in the worker.
-	 */
-	if (srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC]) {
-		struct rkvenc_taskqueue *queue = srv->task_queues[MPP_DEVICE_RKVENC];
-
-		if (queue->kworker_task)
-			kthread_flush_worker(&queue->worker);
-	}
+	rkvenc_service_abort_all(srv);
 
 	if (!wait_event_timeout(srv->quiesce_wait,
 				rkvenc_service_sessions_gone(srv),
@@ -1748,14 +1832,110 @@ void rkvenc_service_quiesce(struct rkvenc_service *srv)
 	if (!srv)
 		return;
 
-	if (rkvenc_service_drain(srv) == RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER)
-		return;
+	if (rkvenc_service_drain(srv) == RKVENC_DRAIN_NOT_OWNER) {
+		/*
+		 * Somebody else owns the LIVE -> QUIESCING transition -- and on
+		 * a service-node unbind that somebody is ALWAYS the first core,
+		 * because __device_release_driver() runs
+		 * device_links_unbind_consumers() before the supplier's own
+		 * device_remove(). So this is the NORMAL path here, not an edge
+		 * case, and returning on it is how remove() came to tear down
+		 * state an open descriptor could still reach without waiting
+		 * even once. Ownership says nothing about whether the
+		 * descriptors are gone; redo the idempotent half rather than
+		 * inherit whatever the other caller happened to do.
+		 */
